@h1 Record-arg h1@

A record-arg is a record literal placed right after a handle —
@@@code @name { field - value, … } code@@@ — whose fields become named
parameters, bound by name to the definition's captures. It is @strong
self-closing strong@ (the matching @@@code } code@@@ ends the node) and it is the
@strong exclusive strong@ form: it will not share a node with any other
parameter source. Reach for it when the arguments read like structured data.

@h2 Fields become parameters h2@

@@@pre
@card { title - Alpha, status - draft }
pre@@@

Both fields bind to the card's captures and render
@code Title Alpha / Status draft code@ (verified). As with records everywhere,
the separator can be a spaced hyphen or a colon:

@@@pre
@card { title: Epsilon, status: draft }
pre@@@

(fixture 22-record-args/inline-single-field, inline-multi-field; the colon form
is 26-all-param-forms/showcase form 6.) The brace binds to explicit captures
(template-expansion) and to captures inferred from the body
(template-implicit-captures) alike.

@h2 Inline or multi-line h2@

The record grammar is identical inline and across lines, so a many-field call
can breathe:

@@@pre
@reference_entry {
  author - Ursula Franklin
  title - The Real World of Technology
  year - 1989
}
pre@@@

Fields are separated by a comma @strong or strong@ a newline; emphasis markers
inside a value are kept literal (fixture 22-record-args/multi-line-record).

@h2 The comma-in-value trap h2@

An @strong unquoted comma splits fields strong@. Because the record parser reads
a comma as a field boundary, a value that contains one is misread as a new field
with no key:

@@@pre
@cite { author - Boud, D., year - 2024 }   ~ E_MALFORMED_RECORD
pre@@@

That is @code E_MALFORMED_RECORD code@ — "record field missing key" (verified).
Quote the value to protect the comma:

@@@pre
@cite { author: "Boud, D.", year: 2024 }   ~ renders: Boud, D. (2024)
pre@@@

(Same root cause as fixture 09-records/comma-in-value.) Quotes also force the
value to be a string.

@h2 Record-arg is exclusive h2@

This is the one form that will not mix. Combining a brace with parens or pipes
collected @strong before strong@ it is a hard error:

@@@pre
@card |status Y| { title X }     ~ E_MIXED_PARAM_SOURCE
@card(status Y) { title X }      ~ E_MIXED_PARAM_SOURCE
pre@@@

Both are @code E_MIXED_PARAM_SOURCE code@ (verified). And a brace @strong
followed strong@ by pipes does not merge either — the record self-closes at
@@@code } code@@@, the trailing pipes are dropped, and the now-missing capture
surfaces downstream:

@@@pre
@card { title: X } |status Y| card@   ~ E_MISSING_RECORD_FIELD
pre@@@

(verified: @code E_MISSING_RECORD_FIELD code@.)

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Leaving a comma inside an unquoted value (quote it). li@
@li Trying to add pipes or parens to the same node (record-arg is exclusive). li@
@li Expecting a body after @@@code { … } code@@@ (it self-closes at
@@@code } code@@@). li@
ul@

